the Tweet was created or not the next

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:18.160 align:start position:19%
endpoint will be for our feed which is
the get to/ feed in the request we can

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:22.560 align:start position:19%
optionally include the cursor of string
which is a pagination token to fetch the

00:04:20.359 --> 00:04:26.680 align:start position:19%
next set of tweets and also from the
server side we will receive this user ID

00:04:24.759 --> 00:04:30.479 align:start position:19%
so that we know which feed we need to
return and in the response we will

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:34.600 align:start position:19%
receive an array of tweet of objects and
each of them will contain media IDs

00:04:32.479 --> 00:04:38.960 align:start position:19%
which is an array of strings of images
and videos and for each of them we will

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:43.240 align:start position:19%
also contain the created at timestamp
and also the next corser which is a

00:04:40.919 --> 00:04:47.560 align:start position:19%
pagination token to fetch the next set
of tweets the next Endo that we will

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:52.120 align:start position:19%
have is for users to be able to follow
each other and that will be a post

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:55.800 align:start position:19%
request to slf SL the ID of the user
that we want to follow and this can be

00:04:54.039 --> 00:04:59.680 align:start position:19%
pretty simple we can just return the
success bullion which indicates whether

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:04.120 align:start position:19%
the follow operation was successful or
or not or we can just return a success

00:05:01.759 --> 00:05:07.440 align:start position:19%
status code and that's it and similar to
this we will have the unfollow endpoint

00:05:06.039 --> 00:05:11.600 align:start position:19%
which is actually not a separate
endpoint but we will just change the

00:05:09.199 --> 00:05:16.000 align:start position:19%
method of this to be delete to slf
follow SL the user ID that we want to

00:05:13.759 --> 00:05:20.199 align:start position:19%
unfollow and now let's move on to the
high level design of this system a quick

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:23.720 align:start position:19%
note if you're enjoying this so far I
have a free system design course in our

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:27.639 align:start position:19%
school Community where you can learn
more about each of the components that

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:31.680 align:start position:19%
we'll be discussing here let's visualize
the major components of this system we

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:35.880 align:start position:19%
will have the mobile app and also the
client and this is where users will

00:05:33.639 --> 00:05:39.880 align:start position:19%
interact with our system creating tweets
viewing feeds and following or

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:43.400 align:start position:19%
unfollowing others in front of all the
requests we will have a load balancer

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:48.479 align:start position:19%
which will distribute the requests
across multiple observers and for now we

00:05:46.199 --> 00:05:52.919 align:start position:19%
can use the layer for load balancing
which operates based on the TCP port

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:56.840 align:start position:19%
numbers and IP addresses and this is
suitable for now for Distributing

00:05:54.919 --> 00:06:00.600 align:start position:19%
General traffic without needing to
inspect the content of the requests but

00:05:59.039 --> 00:06:04.720 align:start position:19%
we'll change the this layer soon in the
scaling section and you'll see exactly

00:06:02.639 --> 00:06:08.599 align:start position:19%
why we need to change it next in front
of this load balancer we will have the

00:06:06.479 --> 00:06:12.520 align:start position:19%
API servers and this will handle the
requests that we created earlier like

00:06:10.599 --> 00:06:16.520 align:start position:19%
fetching tweets filtering feeds or
interacting with the database and cache

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:20.599 align:start position:19%
and rendering responses back to the
client and we also need to decide on the

00:06:18.639 --> 00:06:26.240 align:start position:19%
database whether we are going to use no
SQL or SQL type of database while no SQL

00:06:23.919 --> 00:06:30.880 align:start position:19%
databases will be great here at handling
large amounts of unstructured data but

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:35.440 align:start position:19%
actually a relation database is better
suited for our use case due to the need

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:39.720 align:start position:19%
for complex relationships and Joints
between the users and tweets so we'll

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:44.639 align:start position:19%
use a relational database something like
MySQL or poster SQL to store the

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:49.120 align:start position:19%
structured data regarding our database
schema we will have a followers table

00:06:46.759 --> 00:06:54.199 align:start position:19%
and here we will store the FY ID which
is a string the ID of the user that is

00:06:51.639 --> 00:06:59.080 align:start position:19%
being followed and also the follower ID
which is the ID of the user that follows

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:04.199 align:start position:19%
this followe and we can Index this table
on follower ID to efficiently retrieve

00:07:01.759 --> 00:07:09.000 align:start position:19%
all the people user follows and these
are also foreign Keys which are pointing

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:14.360 align:start position:19%
to the users table in the users table we
will have the basic information like ID

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:18.400 align:start position:19%
of a user username email and also create
that which is the creation date of the

00:07:16.479 --> 00:07:23.000 align:start position:19%
account and from here we will have the
ID as primary key which links to the

00:07:20.599 --> 00:07:27.280 align:start position:19%
tweets table in the tweets table we will
have the Tweet ID which is a unique

00:07:24.919 --> 00:07:31.639 align:start position:19%
identifier for each tweet we will have
the content which is the text content of

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:35.960 align:start position:19%
this tweet and also created at which is
the Tim stamp that the Tweet was created

00:07:34.199 --> 00:07:41.120 align:start position:19%
and here optionally we will also have
the media IDs which is the IDS of the

00:07:38.319 --> 00:07:45.080 align:start position:19%
images videos and other media files and
other than that we may have some small

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:50.199 align:start position:19%
tables like likes which just joins the
tweets and users tables based on the

00:07:47.159 --> 00:07:54.360 align:start position:19%
Tweet ID and user ID and in front of our
database we will have a cache layer

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:58.879 align:start position:19%
something like redis or mcache to store
frequently accessed data like popular

00:07:56.520 --> 00:08:03.720 align:start position:19%
tweets or popular user profiles and this
will reduce the load on our database and

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:07.800 align:start position:19%
it will also improve our read latency
and in our case we can use lru cache

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:11.879 align:start position:19%
which is least recently used and this
will help us keep the most relevant data

00:08:09.919 --> 00:08:16.720 align:start position:19%
in the cache and remove the others that
are not relevant anymore and regarding

00:08:14.319 --> 00:08:21.479 align:start position:19%
the images videos or other media files
we need some sort of object storage

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:25.720 align:start position:19%
something like awss fre or Google Cloud
Storage this is used to store large

00:08:23.479 --> 00:08:29.039 align:start position:19%
media files like the images and videos
that we are going to store and we will

00:08:27.479 --> 00:08:33.680 align:start position:19%
have the associated Tweets in our
database and the link to the media files

00:08:31.479 --> 00:08:37.839 align:start position:19%
which is inside of this object storage
so with this setup the apis will get the

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:42.560 align:start position:19%
tweets from the database and the clients
will query the object storage for the

00:08:39.919 --> 00:08:47.120 align:start position:19%
images and videos but we can take this a
step further and introduce a pool based

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:51.720 align:start position:19%
CDN which will cach our media files
closer to the users geographically and

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:56.080 align:start position:19%
this will further reduce the latency and
it will also improve the user experience

00:08:54.120 --> 00:09:00.240 align:start position:19%
and we'll use a full based CDN which
fetches the content from the origin

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:04.560 align:start position:19%
object storage on the first request and
also caches it for the next incoming

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:08.160 align:start position:19%
requests now with our current system
design the database is the primary

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:12.600 align:start position:19%
bottleneck and if you have something
that will fail that will be the database

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:15.800 align:start position:19%
first so we need to scale our database
first let's see how we can scale the

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:20.200 align:start position:19%
reads to scale the reads we can
introduce read replicas of our primary

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:24.920 align:start position:19%
database and these replicas are just
copies of the primary database but they

00:09:22.399 --> 00:09:29.440 align:start position:19%
are only used for read operations so
we'll have one master replica or leader

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:33.399 align:start position:19%
which is responsible for writing to the
database and we'll have many read

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:38.160 align:start position:19%
replicas or followers which are
responsible for retrieving data to our

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:42.120 align:start position:19%
apis and in addition to these replicas
we also have the caching layer that we

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:46.480 align:start position:19%
are leveraging and popular tweets and
user profiles will be stored there and

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:50.800 align:start position:19%
this will also reduce the need to query
the database for frequently accessed

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:55.200 align:start position:19%
data now let's talk about scaling the
rights as one right database might not

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:59.959 align:start position:19%
be enough for this system and to scale
our right we can sh the database or one

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:04.440 align:start position:19%
specific table like tweet table which
will be used the most based on the user

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:09.240 align:start position:19%
ID and this means that will horizontally
partition the table or database across

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:13.120 align:start position:19%
multiple database servers and each of
these sharts will be responsible for

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:17.120 align:start position:19%
storing the tweets for a specific range
of user IDs and to determine the

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:21.680 align:start position:19%
location of the data will use a
technique called consistent hushing to

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:25.959 align:start position:19%
map the user ID to shards which will
ensure the distribution of data and it

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:30.040 align:start position:19%
will also minimize the impact of adding
or removing shards so we'll add the

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:34.120 align:start position:19%
shards to the database system system and
to further optimize performance we can

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:38.560 align:start position:19%
separate read and write operations
meaning we can have read apis and also

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:43.320 align:start position:19%
write apis and this will be separate
apis each will be responsible for its

00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:47.399 align:start position:19%
own thing so the right API will only be
responsible for writing to the leader

00:10:45.399 --> 00:10:51.959 align:start position:19%
database and the read apis will be
responsible for reading from the cache

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:55.920 align:start position:19%
or from the follower databases and to
achieve this we need to change the layer

00:10:53.839 --> 00:11:00.040 align:start position:19%
of the load balancer we need to change
that to layer seven which is the

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:04.399 align:start position:19%
application layer and this ler allows us
to make routing decisions based on the

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:09.320 align:start position:19%
content of the request itself such as
HTTP methods where we can direct the get

00:11:06.959 --> 00:11:14.360 align:start position:19%
requests to the read apis and the post
delete requests to the write apis or we

00:11:12.040 --> 00:11:18.639 align:start position:19%
can also use the URL path to be more
specific and this is perfect for our

00:11:16.279 --> 00:11:22.880 align:start position:19%
scenario and finally let's also talk
about handling the peak load of our

00:11:20.399 --> 00:11:27.320 align:start position:19%
current system to manage the peak craing
we can introduce a message cue something

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:31.560 align:start position:19%
like Kafka so when a user creates a
tweet the API server will publish a

00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:35.959 align:start position:19%
message to this message queue and we can
have a temporary cache and we can store

00:11:33.639 --> 00:11:40.160 align:start position:19%
the copy of this tweet to the temporary
cache while it's being written to the

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:44.399 align:start position:19%
database and during that time the users
can read this tweet from the temporary

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:49.200 align:start position:19%
cache and once the Tweet will be written
in our database it will also be added to

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:52.920 align:start position:19%
our lru cache and we can remove it from
the temporary cache and this will be the

00:11:51.399 --> 00:11:56.880 align:start position:19%
final look of our high level
architecture obviously Real World

00:11:54.920 --> 00:12:01.120 align:start position:19%
Systems like Twitter are more complex
than this with many challenges like

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:06.000 align:start position:19%
concurrent updates and also the for you
algorithmical feed with pagination twet

00:12:03.560 --> 00:12:09.800 align:start position:19%
ordering and many other challenges but
in real system design interview you

